                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/number+theory+and+physics"} number theory and physics
                      %ul
                        %li
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+hypothesis+and+physics"} Riemann hypothesis and physics
                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/physicscontents"} Edit this sidebar
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#review"} Review
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#original_articles"} Original articles
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            What is called
            %em locally covariant perturbative algebraic quantum field theory
            (
            %a{:href => "#BrunettiFredenhagen00"}> Brunetti-Fredenhagen 00
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#BrunettiFredenhagenVerch03"} Brunetti-Fredenhagen-Verch 03
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+quantum+field+theory"} local
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT+on+curved+spacetimes"} on general spacetimes
            (hence on general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+field"} background field
            configurations of the field theory of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenology
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
          %p
            The approach uses the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+axioms"} Haag-Kastler axioms
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+quantum+field+theory"} algebraic quantum field theory
            in a variant that
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                demands only
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-algebras"} star-algebras
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+observables"} of observables
                instead of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebras"} C*-algebras
              %p
                (this reflects the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
                and the
                = succeed ");" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constant"} coupling constant
            %li
              %p
                evaluates
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+nets+of+observables"} local nets of observables
                not just on causal subspaces of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
                but on more general
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globally+hyperbolic+Lorentzian+manifolds"} globally hyperbolic Lorentzian manifolds
          %p (this reflects the extension to general curved spacetime backgrounds).
          %p
            What connects this to the traditional discussion of QFT is the observation (in essence already due to
            %a{:href => "#IlinSlavnov78"} Il’in-Slavnov 78
            but then ignored) that standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            theory (removal of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultraviolet+divergences"} ultraviolet divergences
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            (
            %a{:href => "#EpsteinGlaser73"}> Epstein-Glaser 73
            ) automatically produces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causally+local+nets"} causally local nets
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+of+observables"} algebras of observables
            (due to “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adiabatic+switching"}> adiabatic switching
            ” and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+locality"} causal locality
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "S-matrix#CausalLocality"} there
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infrared+divergences"} infrared divergences
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+adiabatic+limit"} algebraic adiabatic limit
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+adiabatic+limit"} weak adiabatic limit
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state"} quantum state
          %p
            To put this into perspective, notice that formulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            has many aspects and perspectives. Two almost complementary threads are the following:
          %p
            %strong
              1)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbation theory
              by means of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+geometry"} formal
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagram"} Feynman diagram
              expansions of (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+rotation"}> Wick rotated
              )
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integrals"} path integrals
          %p
            This is the approach predominant in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenology
          %p
            It produces the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/numbers"} numbers
            which are checked to great precision in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiments"} experiments
            starting from the early development of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+electrodynamics"} quantum electrodynamics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+chromodynamics"} quantum chromodynamics
            and recently culminating in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+field"} Higgs field
            physics seen at the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LHC"} LHC
            experiment, confirming the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
          %p
            While many of the mathematical intricacies of this approach have found solutions over the decades, most of these rely on global properties of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            such as translation invariance and existence of an invariant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state"} quantum state
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particles"} particles
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory+on+curved+spacetimes"} quantum field theory on curved spacetimes
            of relevance in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmology"} cosmology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+hole+radiation"} black hole radiation
            or the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instanton+in+QCD"} instanton vacuum of QCD
          %p
            %strong
              2)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+quantum+field+theory"} algebraic quantum field theory
              by means of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+nets"} local nets
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebras"} C*-algebras
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
          %p
            This is the approach predominant in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+physics"} mathematical physics
          %p
            It produces the structural
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theorems"} theorems
            of quantum field theory, such as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/PCT+theorem"} PCT theorem
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin-statistics+theorem"} spin-statistics theorem
            and it seamlessly generalizes to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT+on+curved+spacetimes"} QFT on curved spacetimes
          %p
            In its insistence on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebras"} C*-algebras
            its ambition is to describe the full
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative
            quantum field theory. But as a matter of fact not a single relevant example (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interacting
            QFT in spacetime dimensions 4 or greater) is known. Indeed, the construction of the motivating example,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+of+Yang-Mills+theory"} quantization of Yang-Mills theory
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p Locally covariant perturbative quantum field theory provides a synthesis of these two opposites.
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            Quantum field theory is not only extremely rich in its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenology
            but also its formulation involves a plethora of mathematical tools, such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentzian+geometry"} Lorentzian geometry
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+geometry"} spin geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analysis"} analysis
            (especially
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9chet+manifold"} Fréchet manifold
            theory for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet+bundles"} jet bundles
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+sections"} spaces of sections
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variational+calculus"} variational calculus
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            (specifically
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
            for
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+analysis"} functional analysis
            (specifically
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microlocal+analysis"} microlocal analysis
            for
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+algebra"} operator algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
          %p
            The input datum is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+field+theory"} local
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theory"} Lagrangian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+fields"} backgrounds
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
            from (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globally+hyperbolic+spacetimes"}> globally hyperbolic
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentzian+manifolds"} Lorentzian manifolds
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundles"} field bundles
            over these spacetimes equipped with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lagrangian+density"} local Lagrangian density
            on their
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet+bundle"} jet bundle
          %p
            This induces for each Lorentzian spacetime the corresponding
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+structure"} presymplectic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+space"} smooth space
          %p
            Passage to the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            means to choose a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
            of a subalgebra of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+functions"} algebra of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            on the covariant phase space (the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"}> observables
            ”). This formal deformation is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-algebra"} star-algebra
            whose formal parameter is identified with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
            and it is this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            nature which makes this just a
            %em perturbative
            quantization (while a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+field+theory"} non-perturbative
            quantization would be required to yield a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebra"} C*-algebra
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
          %p
            Finally what makes this
            %em locally covariant
            is that the resulting assignment of formal power series algebras of observables to spacetimes is required to still be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            satisfying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+locality"} causal locality
            (a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net"} local net
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+quantum+field+theory"} algebraic quantum field theory
            in the sense of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+axioms"} Haag-Kastler axioms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebras"} C*-algebras
            it assigns
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-algebras"} star-algebras
          %p In summary, constructing a locally covariant perturbative quantum field theory means to
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                prescribe a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
                encoded by a functor
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mrow
                        %mo {
                        %mtext spacetimes
                        %mo }
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mrow
                        %mo {
                        %mrow
                          %mtable
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mtext field bundles equipped with
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mtext local Lagrangian densities
                        %mo }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      L \;\colon\;\left\{ \text{spacetimes} \right\}
                      \\longrightarrow \left\{ \array{ \text{field bundles equipped with} \\ \text{local Lagrangian densities} }\right\}
              %p
                and consider the induced
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mrow
                        %mo {
                        %mtext spacetimes
                        %mo }
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mtext pre-symplectic smooth spaces
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega \;\colon\; \left\{ \text{spacetimes} \right\} \longrightarrow \{ \text{pre-symplectic smooth spaces} \}
            %li
              %p
                choose a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
                of a subalgebra of the smooth functions on the covariant phase space
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mi obs
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mrow
                        %mo {
                        %mtext spacetimes
                        %mo }
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mrow
                        %mo {
                        %mtext formal power series star algebra
                        %mo }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_{obs} \;\colon\; \left\{ \text{spacetimes} \right\} \longrightarrow \left\{ \text{formal power series star algebra} \right\}
              %p
                such that this satisfies
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+locality"} causal locality
          %p
            While this is straightforward as a prescription, finding any formal deformation quantization on infinite-dimensional phase spaces is highly non-trivial. (Notice that essentially all the existing literature on formal deformation quantization considers it on finite-dimensional manifolds.) But there is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algorithm"} algorithm
            that yields an effective solution together with a parameterization of the available choices, called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                Choose a decomposition of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+density"} Lagrangian density
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                into a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kinetic+action"} kinetic term
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mi kin
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{kin}
                and an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
                term
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi L
                          %mi int
                        %mo ≔
                        %mi L
                        %mo −
                        %msub
                          %mi L
                          %mi kin
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{int} \coloneqq L - L_{kin}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mi kin
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{kin}
                is such that its
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equation"} Euler-Lagrange
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equation+of+motion"} equation of motion
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+operator"} linear
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperbolic+differential+operator"} hyperbolic differential operator
                (the “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+operator"}> wave operator
                ” for the
                = succeed ");" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field"} free field
            %li
              %p
                construct its
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+algebra"} Wick algebra
                which is the vector space of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microcausal+functionals"} microcausal functionals
                on phase space equipped with the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moyal+quantization"} Moyal star product
                induced by the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+Green+function"} causal Green function
                of that hyperbolic differential operator;
            %li
              %p
                for the remaining
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mi int
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{int}
                regarded as an element of the Wick algebra,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"} inductively
                construct its
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
                as a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distribution"} distribution
                with values in the Wick algebra by demanding it to be
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+additivity"} causally additive
                (this is the step of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
                proper). The theorem of (
                %a{:href => "#EpsteinGlaser73"}> Epstein-Glaser 73
                ) states that this has a solution, and that the ambiguity in the solution is at each step of the induction parametrized by a finite set of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
                (given, at stage
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-point+function"} n-point function
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributions"} distributions
                to the
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonal"} diagonal
            %li
              %p
                Finally the desired algebra of observables on some spacetime
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is the Wick algebra on microcausal functions on any slightly larger spacetime
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde X
                deformed by conjugation with the S-matrix evaluated (being a distribution) on any bump function on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde X
                that is unity on
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo ⊂
                        %mover
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \subset \tilde X
          %p
            For
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            this algorithm is due to
            %a{:href => "#EpsteinGlaser73"} Epstein-Glaser 73
            building on ideas of Stückelberg and Bogoliubov, while the generalization to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globally+hyperbolic+spacetimes"} globally hyperbolic spacetimes
            is due to
            %a{:href => "#BrunettiFredenhagen00"} Brunetti-Fredenhagen 00
            (at least for
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+field+theory"} scalar field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net"} local net
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+locality"}> causal locality
            ) was first observed in (
            %a{:href => "#IlinSlavnov78"}> Il’in-Slavnov 78
            ), but ignored or forgotten, and then rediscovered and popularized in (
            %a{:href => "#BrunettiFredenhagen00"}> Brunetti-Fredenhagen 00
            ).
          %p
            When comparing with the bulk of the literature, the reader should beware that most texts do not discuss this algorithm as a means for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            was extracted from experience and justified itself by coinciding with other established recipes of quantum field theory to the extent that these are well-defined, and hence ultimately by producing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QED"} QED
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            which, when truncated to low order in the formal deformation parameter
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℏ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
          %p
            That this algorithm in fact constructs the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
            of the covariant phase space of the given local Lagrangian density
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            was understood only very recently in (
            %a{:href => "#Collini16"}> Collini 16
            ) (for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phi%5E4+theory"} phi^4 theory
            of the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+field"} scalar field
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#HawkinsRejzner16"} Hawkins-Rejzner 16
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+field"} scalar field
            with regular non-local interaction terms).
          %p
            There are choices to be made in finding this deformation quantization. In (
            %a{:href => "#Collini16"}> Collini 16
            ) these are parameterized as the choices of initial conditions in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"} inductive
            construction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fedosov%27s+deformation+quantization"} Fedosov's deformation quantization
            and shown to be equivalent to the choices of causal splitting of distributions in the above algorithm of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            due to
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#EpsteinGlaser73"} Epstein-Glaser 73
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            coefficients in traditional perspectives to perturbative quantum field theory (which in a mathematically rigorous approach might better be called “normalization” coefficients as in
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Scharf95"} Scharf 95, section 4.3
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/main+theorem+of+perturbative+renormalization"} main theorem of perturbative renormalization
            states that the space of these choices is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsor"} torsor
            over a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/St%C3%BCckelberg-Petermann+renormalization+group"} Stückelberg-Petermann renormalization group
          %p
            Finally notice that there is also a choice involved in restricting attention to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microcausal+functionals"} microcausal functionals
            inside the algebra of all smooth functions on phase space. For deformation quantization on finite-dimensional manifolds it is understood that the full algebra of smooth functions is to be quantized, but for the infinite-dimensional phase space of field theory the full algebra of functions does not even support the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            which is to be deformed. The subalgebra of microcausal functionals just happens to be technically under control, closed under the Poisson bracket, and large enough to contain the point-interaction terms that are relevant in quantum field theories observed in nature (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QED"}> QED
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+gravity"} perturbative quantum gravity
          %p
            One way to understand why it is not the full space of smooth functions on phase space that should be quantized is the sibling of algebraic deformation quantization known as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+vector+field"} Hamiltonian observables
            be considered, which are those smooth functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            on phase space such that there exists a smooth vector field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi h
                  %mo =
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d h = \omega(v,-)
            (where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            is the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pre-symplectic+form"} pre-symplectic form
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            is guaranteed to close (for discussion of this in the context of local field theory see
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "Poisson+manifold#BeniniSchenkel16"} Benini-Schenkel 16
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            of the resulting Poisson-bracket
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantomorphism+group"} quantomorphism group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+field+theory"} non-perturbative field theory
            and hence highlights the intrinsic incompleteness of perturbative quantization methods.
          %p
            At the moment, however, non-perturbative quantization of field theories remains completely non-understood (apart from toy cases such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+conformal+field+theories"} 2d conformal field theories
            and certain
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+field+theories"} topological field theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theories"} Yang-Mills theories
            it is one of in the list of open “Millenium Problems” of the Clay Mathematics Institute (
            %a{:href => "quantization+of+Yang-Mills+theory#JaffeWitten"}> Jaffe-Witten
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "quantization+of+Yang-Mills+theory#Douglas04"} Douglas 04
          %p
            For more detailed discussion of all of this see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+A+first+idea+of+quantum+field+theory"} geometry of physics – A first idea of quantum field theory
          %p
            Besides lcpQFT being just perturbative (in
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
          %p
            When applied to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory+on+curved+spacetime"} on curved spacetime
            the usual axioms on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net"} local net
            break: either they enforce all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic+classes"} characteristic classes
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instanton+sectors"}> instanton sectors
            ”) to be trivial, or else the axioms encoding locality are broken (see
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Schenkel14"} Schenkel 14
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/schreiber/show/Higher+field+bundles+for+gauge+fields"} Schreiber 14
          %p
            This remaining problem is meant to be solved by passing from plain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+of+observables"} algebras of observables
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+algebra"} homotopical algebras
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+algebra"}> higher algebras
            ), and hence to a formulation of
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+algebraic+quantum+field+theory"} homotopical algebraic quantum field theory
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Schenkel17"} Schenkel 17
          %h2#references References
          %h3#review Review
          %p
            Survey and exposition of locally covariant algebraic quantum field theory beyond
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            (for which see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "causal+perturbation+theory#References"} there
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#Hollands07"} Hollands 07, section 3
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Wald"}> Robert Wald
                ,
                %em The Formulation of Quantum Field Theory in Curved Spacetime
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0907.0416"}> arXiv:0907.0416
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Wald"}> Robert Wald
                ,
                %em The History and Present Status of Quantum Field Theory in Curved Spacetime
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/gr-qc/0608018"}> arXiv:gr-qc/0608018
                )
            %li#FredenhagenRejzner12
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"}> Klaus Fredenhagen
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Katarzyna+Rejzner"} Katarzyna Rejzner
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Perturbative algebraic quantum field theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Mathematical Aspects of Quantum Field Theories
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1208.1428"} arXiv:1208.1428
            %li#FredenhagenRejzner15
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"}> Klaus Fredenhagen
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Katarzyna+Rejzner"} Katarzyna Rejzner
                %em Perturbative Construction of Models of Algebraic Quantum Field Theory
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.07814"}> arXiv:1503.07814
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"}> Klaus Fredenhagen
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum field theory and gravitation
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://indico.math.cnrs.fr/event/782/material/slides/1.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Rejzner16
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Katarzyna+Rejzner"}> Katarzyna Rejzner
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Perturbative+Algebraic+Quantum+Field+Theory"} Perturbative Algebraic Quantum Field Theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007%2F978-3-319-25901-7"} web
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} geometry of physics – perturbative quantum field theory
            %li#Duetsch18
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"}> Michael Dütsch
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/From+classical+field+theory+to+perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} From classical field theory to perturbative quantum field theory
            %li#BrunettiFredenhagenTejzner23
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Romeo+Brunetti"}> Romeo Brunetti
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kasia+Rejzner"} Kasia Rejzner
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Locally covariant approach to effective quantum gravity
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Quantum+Gravity"} Handbook of Quantum Gravity
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.07800"} arXiv:2212.07800
          %h3#original_articles Original articles
          %p
            The method of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            goes back to ideas of Stückelberg and Bogoliubov. The proof that the causal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            required by this method may always be constructed by an inductive process with a finite-dimensional space of choices (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"}> renormalization
            ”) at each stage is due to
          %ul
            %li#EpsteinGlaser73
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Henri+Epstein"}> Henri Epstein
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Glaser"} Vladimir Glaser
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/The+Role+of+locality+in+perturbation+theory"} The Role of locality in perturbation theory
          %p
            A detailed exposition of this method applied to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QED"} QED
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+gravity"} perturbative quantum gravity
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            is in the textbooks
          %ul
            %li#Scharf95
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%C3%BCnter+Scharf"}> Günter Scharf
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Finite+Quantum+Electrodynamics+--+The+Causal+Approach"} Finite Quantum Electrodynamics – The Causal Approach
            %li#Scharf01
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%C3%BCnter+Scharf"}> Günter Scharf
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quantum+Gauge+Theories+--+A+True+Ghost+Story"} Quantum Gauge Theories – A True Ghost Story
          %p
            The axioms for the locally covariant formulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT+on+curved+spacetimes"} AQFT on curved spacetimes
            is due to
          %ul
            %li#BrunettiFredenhagenVerch03
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Romeo+Brunetti"}> Romeo Brunetti
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rainer+Verch"} Rainer Verch
              = succeed "," do
                %em The generally covariant locality principle – A new paradigm for local quantum physics
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/0112041"} arXiv:math-ph/0112041
          %p
            The construction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+algebras"} Wick algebras
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+fields"} free fields
            on curved
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetimes"} spacetimes
            via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hadamard+distributions"} Hadamard distributions
            relies on the results of
          %ul
            %li#Radzikowski96
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marek+Radzikowski"}> Marek Radzikowski
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Micro-local approach to the Hadamard condition in quantum field theory on curved space-time
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.cmp/1104287114"} Euclid
          %p
            The observation that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            of
            %a{:href => "#EpsteinGlaser73"} Epstein-Glaser 73
            lends itself to a construction of locally covariant perturvative AQFT is due to
          %ul
            %li#IlinSlavnov78
              %p
                V. A. Il’in and D. S. Slavnov,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Observable algebras in the S-matrix approach
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/135575"} spire
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/BF01035870"} doi
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#BrunettiFredenhagen00"} Brunetti-Fredenhagen 00, last section
            %li#BrunettiDuetschFredenhagen09
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Romeo+Brunetti"}> Romeo Brunetti
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"} Michael Dütsch
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Perturbative Algebraic Quantum Field Theory and the Renormalization Groups
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0901.2038"} arXiv:0901.2038
          %p
            Extension of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT+on+curved+spacetimes"} curved spacetimes
            is due to
          %ul
            %li#BrunettiFredenhagen00
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Romeo+Brunetti"}> Romeo Brunetti
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Microlocal Analysis and Interacting Quantum Field Theories: Renormalization on Physical Backgrounds
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/9903028"} math-ph/9903028
              %p following
              %ul
                %li#Stora93
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Raymond+Stora"}> Raymond Stora
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Differential algebras in Lagrangean field theory
            %li#DuetschFredenhagen00
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"}> Michael Dütsch
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Algebraic Quantum Field Theory, Perturbation Theory, and the Loop Expansion
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0001129"} arXiv:hep-th/0001129
            %li#HollandsWald01
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefan+Hollands"}> Stefan Hollands
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Wald"} Robert Wald
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Local Wick Polynomials and Time Ordered Products of Quantum Fields in Curved Spacetime
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/gr-qc/0103074"} arXiv:gr-qc/0103074
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefan+Hollands"}> Stefan Hollands
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Wald"} Robert Wald
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the Renormalization Group in Curved Spacetime
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/gr-qc/0209029"} arXiv:gr-qc/0209029
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefan+Hollands"}> Stefan Hollands
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Wald"} Robert Wald
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Conservation of the stress tensor in perturbative interacting quantum field theory in curved spacetimes
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/gr-qc/0404074"} arXiv:gr-qc/0404074
          %p
            Specifically construction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            on curved spacetimes is due to
          %ul
            %li#Hollands07
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefan+Hollands"}> Stefan Hollands
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Renormalized Quantum Yang-Mills Fields in Curved Spacetime
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0705.3340"} arXiv:0705.3340
          %p
            The observation that this construction produces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
            of the classical field theory was made for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field"} free field
            and its quantization by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+algebras"} Wick algebras
            is
          %ul
            %li
              J. Dito,
              = succeed "." do
                %em Star-product approach to quantum field theory: The free scalar field
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://inspirehep.net/record/303898/"} spire
          %p which was amplified in
          %ul
            %li#DuetschFredenhagen01
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"}> Michael Dütsch
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
              = succeed "," do
                %em Perturbative algebraic field theory, and deformation quantization
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Roberto+Longo"} Roberto Longo
              (ed.),
              = succeed "," do
                %em Mathematical Physics in Mathematics and Physics, Quantum and Operator Algebraic Aspects
              %p
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0101079"}> arXiv:hep-th/0101079
                )
          %p
            and generalized to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory+on+curved+spacetimes"} quantum field theory on curved spacetimes
            in
            %a{:href => "#BrunettiFredenhagen00"} Brunetti-Fredenhagen 00
            and
          %ul
            %li#BrunettiFredenhagen95
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Romeo+Brunetti"}> Romeo Brunetti
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The microlocal spectrum condition and Wick polynomials on curved spacetimes
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/gr-qc/9510056"} arXiv:gr-qc/9510056
            %li#HollandsWald01
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefan+Hollands"}> Stefan Hollands
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Wald"} Robert Wald
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Local Wick Polynomials and Time Ordered Products of Quantum Fields in Curved Spacetime
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/gr-qc/0103074"} arXiv:gr-qc/0103074
          %p
            That more generally the deformation quantization of interacting fields yields the traditional prescription of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            was shown for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+field"} scalar field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phi%5E4+theory"} phi^4 theory
            in
          %ul
            %li#Collini16
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giovanni+Collini"}> Giovanni Collini
              ,
              %em Fedosov Quantization and Perturbative Quantum Field Theory
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.09626"}> arXiv:1603.09626
              )
          %p and for the interacting scalar field in the toy example of regular non-local interactions in
          %ul
            %li#HawkinsRejzner16
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eli+Hawkins"}> Eli Hawkins
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kasia+Rejzner"} Kasia Rejzner
              %em The Star Product in Interacting Quantum Field Theory
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.09157"}> arXiv:1612.09157
              )
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantization
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theories"} gauge theories
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"}> Yang-Mills theory
            ) in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+AQFT"} perturbative AQFT
            is discussed (for trivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundles"} principal bundles
            and restricted to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+invariant+observables"} gauge invariant observables
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BRST-complex"} BRST-complex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-formalism"} BV-formalism
            in
          %ul
            %li#FredenhagenRejzner11a
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"}> Klaus Fredenhagen
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kasia+Rejzner"} Kasia Rejzner
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Batalin-Vilkovisky formalism in the functional approach to classical field theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1101.5112"} arXiv:1101.5112
            %li#FredenhagenRejzner11b
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"}> Klaus Fredenhagen
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kasia+Rejzner"} Kasia Rejzner
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Batalin-Vilkovisky formalism in perturbative algebraic quantum field theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1110.5232"} arXiv:1110.5232
          %p and surveyed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#Rejzner16"} Rejzner 16, chapter 7
          %p
            A survey of the required generalization for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            with non-trivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instanton+sectors"} instanton sectors
            via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+algebraic+quantum+field+theory"} homotopical algebraic quantum field theory
            is in
          %ul
            %li#Schenkel14
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Schenkel"}> Alexander Schenkel
                ,
                %em On the problem of gauge theories
              %p
                in locally covariant QFT_, talk at
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://www.science.unitn.it/~moretti/convegno/convegno.html"} Operator and Geometric Analysis on Quantum Theory
                Trento, 2014 (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/SchenkelTrento2014.pdf", :title => "pdf"}> pdf
                ) (with further emphasis on this point in the companion talk
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "field+bundle#Schreiber14"} Schreiber 14
            %li#Schenkel17
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Schenkel"}> Alexander Schenkel
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Towards Homotopical Algebraic Quantum Field Theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "https://indico.mitp.uni-mainz.de/event/76/overview"} Foundational and Structural Aspects of Gauge Theories
              %p
                Mainz Institute for Theoretical Physics, 29 May – 2 June 2017. (
                %a{:href => "http://aschenkel.eu/Mainz17.pdf"}> pdf
                )
